estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: "Absolute-pattern detection uses glob syntax (a leading / or a drive prefix), never win32 path.isAbsolute on the raw pattern; tests/unit/core/fs/file-glob.spec.ts passes on windows-latest, with the leading-backslash parity entry POSIX-only and a cross-platform escape test; The Windows nightly shard that runs file-glob.spec.ts is green"
parent: pm-i7w2
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
expected_result: "A pattern with a leading backslash is read as a glob escape on every platform, so \\\\a.ts matches a.ts relative to cwd and \\\\!x.ts matches the file !x.ts, with the same relative output on Windows and POSIX."
actual_result: "On Windows, \\\\a.ts and \\\\!x.ts return absolute workspace paths (for example C:/.../!x.ts), because win32 path.isAbsolute treats the leading backslash as root-relative while the walk still starts at cwd."
